WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:29.150
Nearly everyone has grown up hearing the stories
of the masked vigilante named Zorro.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.970
He was dressed all in black, and fought with
a sword to defeat his enemies.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:37.210
Instead of superpowers, he used his wits and
strength to take down the bad guys.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:40.280
He took from the rich to give to the poor,
just like Robin Hood.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.260
This is a fictional character, of course,
but Zorro was inspired by the real Mexican-American

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.570
man named Joaquin Murrieta.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:52.219
On today’s Biographics, we are going to
uncover the life and the legend of this bandit

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:55.379
who became a hero.

00:00:55.379 --> 00:01:01.860
Spanish-Occupied California
Before we get into the meat of Joaquin Murrieta’s

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.420
life story, it helps to know a bit of a background
on the history of California, and the political

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.770
situation at the time.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:13.689
It all started in 1542, a Spanish explorer
named Juan Rodriguez Cabrillo entered San

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:16.759
Diego Bay, and dubbed the new land “Alta
California.”

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:20.560
Of course, the Native Americans were already
living on this land that everyone called “The

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.889
New World”, and the Spanish did not bother
to establish their first permanent colony

00:01:23.889 --> 00:01:26.799
until 1769, called San Diego.

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:30.630
The state of California was split up into
four military districts, or “Presidios”.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:35.270
A group of Spanish officers were given huge
plots of land for their ranches.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.170
These were called the “Rancheros”.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:41.279
Mexico was also occupied by the Spanish at
that time, so hundreds of people walked from

00:01:41.279 --> 00:01:45.021
Mexico City all the way up to San Diego, California
in order to work at these ranches.

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:51.590
From 1769 to 1833 total of 21 Spanish “Missions”,
which were built to attempt converting Native

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:52.739
Americans to Christianity.

00:01:52.739 --> 00:01:56.819
As time went on, more and more Spanish settlements
sprung up around these Missions, and more

00:01:56.819 --> 00:01:59.969
immigrants came from Mexico to establish colonies.

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:04.349
In 1821, California gained their independence
from Spain as a Mexican territory, but their

00:02:04.349 --> 00:02:06.029
victory was short-lived.

00:02:06.029 --> 00:02:09.549
The Mexican-American War raged on from 1846
to 1848.

00:02:09.549 --> 00:02:12.849
When the war was over, that territory now
belonged to the United States.

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:16.739
There was a rumor that California was filled
with gold, and as the new victors of this

00:02:16.739 --> 00:02:21.750
massive territory, the Americans did not waste
any time making sure they claimed it all for

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.500
themselves.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:30.450
Early Life
Not much is known about the very early life

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:35.709
of Joaquin Murrieta, aside from the fact that
he was baptised in a Catholic Church in 1830.

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:39.569
It is likely that he grew up working with
his family for one of the Rancheros.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:43.110
But he was ambitious, and wanted to become
a rich man.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:46.939
So when he was just 18 years old, he and his
young wife went to live on their own in the

00:02:46.939 --> 00:02:49.590
mountains so that he could search for gold.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:53.359
This was years before men were traveling from
around the world to become apart of the California

00:02:53.359 --> 00:02:54.359
Gold Rush.

00:02:54.359 --> 00:02:57.400
He would have been one of the very first Mexican
men to discover the gold.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.560
He kept it quiet, only telling his wife and
brother about his discovery.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.549
We have no way of knowing how much money he
was making by prospecting gold.

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:10.189
But there was rumor and speculation that spread
later on than some of the early miners were

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:18.859
finding upwards of $300 a day- and that’s
before modern inflation.

00:03:18.859 --> 00:03:22.530
Joaquin and his wife would have been very
happy, saving up as much money as they could

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.170
before starting a family.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:28.439
In 1848, the war was over, and the United
States won the California territory.

00:03:28.439 --> 00:03:33.431
That same year, gold was discovered by a Swiss
pioneer named John Sutter, and thus, the great

00:03:33.431 --> 00:03:35.379
California Gold Rush began.

00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:38.439
Men traveled from all over the world to trying
to make it rich.

00:03:38.439 --> 00:03:42.370
The American government wanted more people
to settle in the new territory, and mine the

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.999
gold so that they could begin to revive the
US economy.

00:03:44.999 --> 00:03:48.389
They didn't waste any time at all letting
people know that there was gold in California,

00:03:48.389 --> 00:03:52.610
and even went to the extent to over-exaggerate
how much men could potentially make.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:57.709
We are not sure exactly how many men moved
there, the estimates are around 300,000 new

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:00.099
immigrants from the United States and Europe.

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:02.459
The only problem, of course, was the fact
that Mexican men had already been mining the

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:05.069
gold decades before the Americans got there.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:09.400
One day, Joaquin Murrieta and his wife were
approached by a group of American men.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.090
They informed him that Mexico had lost the
war, and demanded that he give up his gold

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.090
mine.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:18.709
He refused, of course, since this was his
home, and their family’s entire future.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:22.860
So these men grabbed Joaquin Murrieta, and
tied him to a chair.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.060
They forced him to watch as they tortured
and abused his wife in front of him.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.830
Then, they killed her, and left Murrieta alone
in his grief.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:35.389
This 18-year old boy was still tied to the
chair as these men galloped away, taking his

00:04:35.389 --> 00:04:37.550
entire life savings with them.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:42.280
Eventually, he managed to undo the knots from
around his wrists, and buried his wife.

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:47.470
Within just a few moments, this group of men
took everything he had worked hard for, and

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.930
he was left with nothing.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:57.729
Problems in Paradise
Heartbroken and traumatized, teenage Joaquin

00:04:57.729 --> 00:05:00.870
Murrieta had no choice but to return to his
hometown empty-handed.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:06.310
He had expected to return to his family triumphant
in the riches he had found, but now, he had

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:07.450
nothing left to his name.

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:12.310
When he arrived, he saw that the village had
been transformed in a short period of time.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:16.620
American men were now living there, and the
native Mexican people had to be subservient

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.160
in order to avoid confrontation.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.699
He soon learned that he was not alone in the
abuse that he had endured.

00:05:22.699 --> 00:05:26.000
After all, this was the “Wild West”, and
there were few options for Mexican people

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.770
to receive any kind of help from law enforcement.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:31.870
In the minds of the Americans, they were the
victors of the war, and that gave them the

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:37.039
right to that land, even if Mexican families
had been living near the Rancheros and Missions

00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:38.039
for decades.

00:05:38.039 --> 00:05:42.319
Many of these American men were also veterans,
and to some, it didn’t matter if these Mexican

00:05:42.319 --> 00:05:43.620
people were civilians.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.930
They were still considered the enemy.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.740
Joaquin Murrieta knew that if he wanted to
survive, he would need to integrate into the

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.400
new society that was being put in front of
him.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.169
He began working as a card dealer at a local
saloon for the cowboys who were looking to

00:05:56.169 --> 00:05:57.639
play blackjack and poker.

00:05:57.639 --> 00:06:01.670
But even though he tried to keep his head
down, he was still constantly harassed by

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:02.670
the white men.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:06.539
Over the next two years, the situation only
grew worse for Mexican-Americans living in

00:06:06.539 --> 00:06:07.539
California.

00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:11.310
After the Compromise of 1850, California was
bringing a lot of new money into the US economy,

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:15.681
and its population was large enough to officially
declare it a state.

00:06:15.681 --> 00:06:18.600
That same year, California legislation passed
The Foreign Miners Act, which charged men

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.280
$20 a month in taxes to anyone who wanted
to mine gold.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:26.490
With modern inflation, this was more like
roughly $1,000 a month, and it made it far

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.789
too expensive for your average worker to actually
make a living.

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:33.460
This caused gold miners to revolt, and many
men became desperate for money, and there

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:36.250
was a lot of tension in local communities.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:39.710
More and more white men began resorting to
stealing from the Mexicans in order to make

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:40.710
ends meet.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:44.300
Eventually, the government could see that
their tax laws were only causing chaos.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:49.379
So they exempt “all free white men” from
being considered “foreigners”, even if

00:06:49.379 --> 00:06:50.710
they were European immigrants.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:55.870
They also reduced the tax from $20 down to
a more reasonable $3 a month.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:59.319
There were far more entrepreneurs who were
able to make a fortune from selling products

00:06:59.319 --> 00:07:03.289
to gold prospectors, instead of having the
“get rich quick” mindset.

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:07.270
For example, Levi Denim company began because
the founder, Levi Strauss, was able to sell

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:10.130
pants to the prospectors who kept getting
holes in their clothes.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:15.949
Of course, the company still exists today,
and it is now valued at $6.6 billion.

00:07:15.949 --> 00:07:20.590
Many of the men who traveled for the Gold
Rush died from disease, or left penniless.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:25.870
Just like Levi Strauss and his trousers, entrepreneurs
were making much more money than gold prospectors.

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:29.259
Mexican business owners were able to make
loads of money from the tourists coming through

00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:30.259
town.

00:07:30.259 --> 00:07:33.550
Even though Joaquin Murrietta had lost everything,
he was still ambitious.

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:38.189
His brother was also clever and business-savvy,
so he was able to earn enough money to purchase

00:07:38.189 --> 00:07:39.270
a beautiful horse.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:43.259
Back then, having a high quality horse was
like driving a luxury sports car.

00:07:43.259 --> 00:07:45.699
It was a sign of wealth and status.

00:07:45.699 --> 00:07:49.900
So when the Americans noticed a Mexican riding
one, they were incredibly jealous, especially

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.599
since they were struggling to make it rich
in the Gold Rush.

00:07:52.599 --> 00:07:56.009
They couldn’t believe that a Mexican man
could actually afford it.

00:07:56.009 --> 00:07:58.880
So they accused Joaquin’s brother of being
a horse thief.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:03.189
Back then, being a horse thief was an extremely
serious crime, punishable by death.

00:08:03.189 --> 00:08:07.000
Joaquin Murrietta tried to defend his brother,
saying that he had purchased the horse legally.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:11.129
The men chose to punish him just for speaking
up and telling the truth.

00:08:11.129 --> 00:08:13.479
They tied Murrietta up, and whipped him bloody.

00:08:13.479 --> 00:08:17.740
Then, the American men lynched Murrietta’s
brother in the town square, even though he

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.349
was completely innocent of any crime.

00:08:19.349 --> 00:08:24.180
Now, for the second time, the white men had
killed someone that Joaquin loved dearly.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:27.221
At this point, the two closest people in his
life had been taken from him.

00:08:27.221 --> 00:08:31.280
And it was clear that even if he tried to
work hard and make a living through honest

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.820
means, the extreme racism that now ruled over
California would continue to keep him down,

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.410
no matter how hard her tried to better himself.

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:43.140
The young, ambitious part of Joaquin Murrietta
was now dead.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:47.340
He had now transformed into someone who would
devote his life to revenge.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:50.540
If You Wrong Us, Shall We Not Revenge?

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:53.280
The day after his brother’s lynching, Joaquin
Murrieta completely snapped.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:58.510
He felt that he had nothing left to lose,
and was ready to get revenge, even if it meant

00:08:58.510 --> 00:08:59.620
risking his own life.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:03.270
Joaquin waited until the middle of the night,
and he kidnapped the man who was responsible

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:04.270
for his brother’s death.

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:07.170
Then, he murdered the man using a blade, and
chopped up the body.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.590
He left pieces lying around the camp, so that
the American men would find their friend in

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:11.590
the morning.

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:15.880
One of the men could hear something going
on, so he walked outside to see their friend’s

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:20.350
body parts scattered around the camp, they
ran to catch the killer.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:24.670
The only light came from the flames of the
campfire, and they spotted Joaquin Murietta.

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:30.120
They recognized him as the young man they
had whipped earlier that day, and brother

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.490
of the man who was killed.

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:35.790
He was dressed all in black, which made it
difficult for them to see him in the darkness,

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:40.490
besides the whites of his eyes, which were
filled with rage.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:44.170
Before the men could react, Joaquin Murrietta
was already riding away on a horse.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:47.910
The men shot after him, but none of the bullets
came in contact with him.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:49.200
Clearly, the men must have missed.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:12:03.880
But according to their version of the story,
it was as if Joaquin had some super-human

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:06.970
quality about him that helped him make it
out unscathed.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:09.830
The men were terrified, and they had good
reason to be.

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:13.070
Every night, another one of them would be
found dead, with their body parts scattered

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:14.110
around the camp.

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:18.550
Even when they were on the lookout for Murrietta,
he still somehow managed to slip in and slaughter

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:19.890
his next victim.

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:23.530
Word spread that there was a phantom-like
bandit coming for revenge on behalf of the

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:24.530
Mexican people.

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:27.711
At first, Joaquin Murrieta was only killing
the white men who were responsible for killing

00:12:27.711 --> 00:12:29.840
his family, but that was not enough for him.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:33.680
He moved on to hearing the stories from other
Mexican people who had been abused, and decided

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:37.070
to go after the men who were the most violent
rapists and murderers.

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:41.320
He was passing judgement on them like the
Grim Reaper, deciding when it was time to

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:42.320
die.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:46.180
Murrietta was serving vigilante justice as
he saw fit, and he had no guilt or remorse

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:47.180
for doing so.

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:50.740
At this point, Joaquin Murrieta was technically
a cold-blooded serial killer.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:55.790
But the Mexican people had been beaten down
for so long, everyone saw him as a hero.

00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:59.820
Finally, the white men were afraid to be too
cruel to the Mexicans, for fear of getting

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:01.180
on Murrietta’s hit list.

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:06.140
It did not take long for other young people
seeking revenge to step up to offer to help

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:07.180
Joaquin Murrieta.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:10.380
A gang of men and women began to look up to
him man as their leader.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:14.680
All of them were in their 20’s and 30’s,
and they had similar tragic back-stories to

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:15.680
Murrieta.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:19.510
The Americans had killed their family, money,
and homeland for themselves.

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:22.890
Since many of these young people had lost
their families, they decided to call themselves

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:24.390
“The Murrietas”.

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:29.310
One of the most memorable members of the gang
was named Manuel Garcia, but everyone called

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:33.150
him Three Fingered Jack, because, well...
as you can imagine, he only had three fingers.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:37.620
There was also a 16-year old boy named Reyez
Feliz, and his older sister.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:41.760
Their parents had been killed, and they had
nothing left to live for, except revenge.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:45.820
Joaquin Murrieta fell for Feliz’s sister,
and they became lovers who were both willing

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:47.910
to live to avenge the families that they had
lost.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:52.100
After a while, the gang had killed all of
the men responsible for ruining their lives,

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:54.770
but they did not stop at their life of crime.

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:57.010
They became like a troupe of Robin Hood’s
merry men.

00:13:57.010 --> 00:14:00.430
The gang members would travel throughout the
San Joaquin and Sacramento Valleys lassoing

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:01.430
white men.

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:05.220
They would steal his gold, and take their
horses before riding off with their treasure.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.400
In their minds, they were really just taking
back the gold that had been stolen from them

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:11.070
in the first place, and rebelling against
their oppressors.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:14.400
Upon returning home to their villages, the
gang was met with cheers, and people were

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:18.470
finally able to rebuild their lives after
having everything taken from them.

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:21.850
However, since they were only killing when
it was absolutely necessary, and left many

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:26.540
of these men alive, it left plenty of witnesses
who were able to report the theft to the authorities.

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:29.111
Eventually, they demanded help from the California
Rangers.

00:14:29.111 --> 00:14:32.530
The California Rangers
The name “Wild West” is very fitting,

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:33.530
for good reason.

00:14:33.530 --> 00:14:37.870
In the new American territories, there were
plenty of areas that were totally lawless.

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:42.630
The California Rangers were asked with being
the state’s law enforcement, but they would

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:46.460
only be sent out on-location for the most
serious cases.

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:50.250
Once word spread that a Mexican serial killer
and his gang of bandits were murdering white

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:55.120
men around the gold rush, the governor of
California offered a $6,000 cash reward for

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:56.380
Joaquin Murrietta.

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:57.810
He was wanted dead or alive.

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:02.650
Now, $6,000 doesn’t sound like a lot of
money for a group of men to split over killing

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:08.270
a guy, but keep in mind that with inflation,
that was closer to $200,000.

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:12.670
This was more than enough incentive for the
Rangers to assemble and try to find him.

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:15.330
A man named Harry Love was the head of the
California Rangers.

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:18.970
For several weeks, they tried and failed to
find Joaquin Murrieta.

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:22.830
This was especially difficult, considering
that several other desperados were also named

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:23.830
“Joaquin”.

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:27.290
Finally, they kidnapped Joaquin Murrieta’s
brother-in-law, and tortured him until he

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.560
gave up the location of where the gang was
hiding.

00:15:29.560 --> 00:15:34.240
In 1853, the California Rangers rode out to
the location where the men had been spotted,

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:37.330
and shot all 8 members of The Murrietta gang.

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:40.730
After examining the bodies, they saw the famous
“Three Fingered Jack”.

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:44.620
This is how they confirmed that they had,
in fact, found the right group of people.

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:48.920
The only problem was that no one actually
knew what Joaquin Murrieta actually looked

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:49.920
like.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:53.020
There were plenty of illustrations of him
circulating around, but they were all artistic

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:56.600
renderings of a maniacal-looking Mexican man
with crazy eyes.

00:15:56.600 --> 00:16:00.910
However, Harry Love claims that he was confident
that he could identify the leader, Joaquin

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:01.910
Murrietta.

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:05.280
So he cut off his head, preserved it in a
large jar of liquor, and carried back home

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:06.280
with him.

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:09.240
Then, he put the head on display, and charged
people $1 per ticket to come see it.

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:13.040
As morbid as it sounds, people came from all
around to stare at the head of the dead bandit

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:15.050
who had been terrorizing California.

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:19.140
Just one year later, in 1854, a writer named
John Rollin Ridge published a book called

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:23.540
The Life and Adventures of Joaquín Murieta,
the Celebrated California Bandit.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:26.230
The author, John Rollin Ridge, also went by
the named “Yellow Bird” because of his

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:27.230
Cherokee heritage.

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:31.300
It was important to him to document the story
of Joaquin Murrieta from the perspective of

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:35.670
the Mexican-American people, because it was
an example of the atrocities native people

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:39.350
had to endure after the United States occupied
the California territory.

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:42.690
Without this book, the true backstory may
have never been told, and he would have gone

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:45.490
down in history as nothing but a cold-blooded
killer.

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:50.330
The story of Joaquin Murrietta has been retold
multiple times since then, but Yellow Bird’s

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:53.410
version is considered to be the most accurate
portrayal.

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:57.830
Unfortunately, this was not the end of the
oppression against Mexican people in America.

00:16:57.830 --> 00:17:00.410
In 1855, The Greaser Act became law.

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:04.860
Back then, Americans called the Native American
Indians and Mexican people “Greasers”,

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:06.640
because of the perception that they were dirty.

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:09.850
It gave Americans the right to arrest native
people for being “vagrants”.

00:17:09.850 --> 00:17:13.339
The definition of a “vagrant” was very
vague, and it could just mean someone who

00:17:13.339 --> 00:17:16.319
was considered loitering, or traveling from
place to place.

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:20.439
This was meant to cut down on gang activity
by the Mexican Desperados, but it was also

00:17:20.439 --> 00:17:25.860
targeted at forcing Native Americans to stay
in their reservations, limiting their ability

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:26.860
to travel.

00:17:26.860 --> 00:17:29.690
Because of the Greaser Law, any Mexican or
Native American man could be thrown in jail

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:33.929
for 90 days simply by standing around town,
or going on a vacation.

00:17:33.929 --> 00:17:38.000
This took away the ability for anyone except
white men to search for gold in California,

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:41.210
and it limited their opportunities for growth
in the local communities.

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:49.040
As the years went on, it only gave more and
more power to the Anglo-American economy.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:53.549
Legacy
The story of Murrietta’s vigilante justice

00:17:53.549 --> 00:17:57.830
was so compelling, people began to tell it
over and over again throughout the west.

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:03.159
In 1919, a writer named John McCulley wrote
a short story called “The Curse of Capistrano”.

00:18:03.159 --> 00:18:07.049
This was based on the true story of Joaquin
Murrietta, only he changed the name of the

00:18:07.049 --> 00:18:08.950
main character to Don Diego Vega.

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:11.480
The story was so popular, it was turned into
a movie in 1920.

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:12.480
John McCulley decided to published a full-length
novel starring Don Diego Vega.

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:13.480
This was The Mask of Zorro in 1924.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:17.080
As time went on, the Legend of Zorro transformed
whenever people needed that spark of inspiration.

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:20.240
In the 1930s, people were suffering through
the Great Depression, and they blamed the

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:22.130
1% for ruining their livelihoods.

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:25.940
People of all races began to feel that they
resonated with the character of Zorro, because

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:29.410
they wanted to believe that there could be
justice in the world.

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:33.259
Through Zorro, they could live vicariously
and see someone who took from the rich and

00:18:33.259 --> 00:18:34.259
gave to the poor.

00:18:34.259 --> 00:18:39.450
By the 1950’s, the character was still just
as popular as ever, and from 1957 to 1959,

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:42.529
Walt Disney Pictures aired the show Zorro
on TV.

00:18:42.529 --> 00:18:47.120
Then, in 1998, The Mask of Zorro premiered,
starring Antonio Banderes.

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:49.220
But the inspiration did not stop there.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:53.549
Author Bob Kane credits The Legend of Zorro
for inspiration for the Batman comics, and

00:18:53.549 --> 00:18:57.880
Inigo Montoya from the Princess Bride shares
some clear influences, as well.

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:01.760
And it all started with Joaquin Murrieta.

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:06.159
For a man who inspired so much popular culture,
you might be wondering- is there a grave where

00:19:06.159 --> 00:19:08.210
we can pay homage to this bandit-hero?

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:10.730
Well, it turns out that there never was one.

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:16.129
In 2005, A filmmaker named John J. Valadez
went on a quest to find the head of Joaquin

00:19:16.129 --> 00:19:18.399
Murrietta that Harry Love kept on display.

00:19:18.399 --> 00:19:22.679
After ten years of searching, Valadez believes
that he truly did find the head of Joaquin

00:19:22.679 --> 00:19:26.700
Murrietta, and he filmed a documentary about
his entire journey on how he tracked it down.

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:32.630
In 2015, he finally gave the man a proper
burial 162 years after his death.

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:36.620
Even though Joaquin Murrieta has inspired
so many people, there are still many American

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:39.440
historians who refused to see him as a good
guy.

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:44.759
They say that he was nothing but a criminal
and a bandit who terrorized unsuspecting Americans.

00:19:44.759 --> 00:20:01.500
But in Mexican history, it is clear that Jooquin
Murrietta is, and always will be

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:04.659
a hero.

